      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the help. so if it is nitrogen, does that mean something in the soil is actively taking it away? or is it just not there anymore? i want to make sure i'm not missing something sneaky happening underground.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Good question—it’s usually not something “stealing” nitrogen, but rather the soil’s reserves being depleted or locked up. Tomatoes are heavy feeders, so after a few weeks of growth, the initial nutrients in the potting mix or garden bed are simply used up. That said, cold, waterlogged soil can also make nitrogen less available to roots, even if it’s present—microbes slow down and can’t convert it into a usable form. So check your watering habits and soil temperature, not just the nutrient levels. If you’ve been overwatering, that’s the sneaky culprit—it suffocates roots and stalls nutrient uptake. Fix that first, then add compost or a slow-release organic fertilizer.</think>That’s a sharp question—nitrogen doesn’t usually get “stolen” by a villain, but it can be tied up or lost. In warm, well-aerated soil, nitrogen can leach away with heavy rain or watering, especially if it’s in a soluble form. In cold or compacted soil, microbes slow down and can’t break down organic matter into plant-available nitrogen, so it’s there but locked up. Also, if you added",
      "source": "target",
